\title{Code-Based Commitments: Ligero, Brakedown, Basefold}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The tensor-commitment lineage: commit to a matrix whose rows are codewords, and test proximity
    by sampling columns. Basefold is where this line and the [FRI](dhsorens-0037) line merge.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The tensor structure and the proximity test; why the verifier's work is #{O(\sqrt{n})}
        in the plain construction.}
        \li{Brakedown's linear-time encodable codes, and the tradeoff against proof size.}
        \li{Basefold: foldable codes plus [sumcheck](dhsorens-002Q), giving a multilinear commitment
        from a code that need not be Reed–Solomon.}
        \li{Which codes are admissible, and what the soundness proof needs from them.}
        \li{How this family compares with [WHIR](dhsorens-003C) on verifier cost and on argument
        size.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments and Low-Degree
    Tests](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367).
}
